72
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:08,080
Um you should always let a laser head warm up before you do a hologram because you're allowing it to reach thermal stability.

73
00:04:08,080 --> 00:04:14,640
But once that laser head warms up, it shouldn't be anywhere near where it was where it would feel very hot to the touch.

74
00:04:14,640 --> 00:04:21,440
I picked up a thermometer at Radio Shack, and this is an indoor-outdoor thermometer.

75
00:04:21,440 --> 00:04:34,480
And what I do is I have that thermometer on the wall next to the laser, and there's a probe, and you use this probe in normal circumstances by putting it out your window so that you can tell what the outside temperature is.

76
00:04:34,480 --> 00:04:40,800
What I did is I took the probe and I got one of those metal collars that you can buy over at the hardware store.

77
00:04:41,040 --> 00:04:45,840
Sort of like holds hoses, a hose clamp, I think is what it's called.

78
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:49,680
And you can s it has a s uh screw on it and you can make it tighter and tighter.

79
00:04:49,680 --> 00:04:51,600
It makes the collar smaller and smaller.

80
00:04:51,600 --> 00:05:02,400
Well, I slip that over the laser head, I put the little temperature probe underneath of it, and then I screw that down so that the temperature probe is touching the laser head itself.

81
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:16,480
This way I can tell number one how hot the laser head's getting, and I can watch the temperature and see when it finally reaches a point where it's not rising or dropping anymore, and that means of course that the laser has reached thermal stability.

82
00:05:16,480 --> 00:05:21,920
The temperature that I usually see on there, I believe, is one hundred and one degrees.

83
00:05:21,920 --> 00:05:23,360
